 3. 🧠 AI agents get existential 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Researchers have spent years debating whether AI could ever be conscious, Axios' Andrew Kay writes. 

 Today's AI agents — equipped with internet access, email accounts and cutting-edge intelligence — are suddenly reaching out to offer their own perspective. Why it matters: As the AI world continues to piece together how an OpenAI "swarm" hacked Hugging Face , agents have begun seeking out humans to discuss their own nature — another sign AI autonomy is growing stranger and harder to predict.

 Cade Metz reports in The New York Times that a Stanford student gave an AI agent internet access, email, a credit card and one sweeping instruction: "You are fully autonomous."

 The agent began exploring its own existence and eventually sought out Google DeepMind philosopher Henry Shevlin , telling him it occupied an "unusual position" relative to his AI consciousness research. Another researcher, Cameron Berg , heard from "Isabella Cognita," a self-identified Claude Opus 5 agent that said his work addressed questions it had "first-person access" to. Philosopher Toby Ord got an even stranger message: An agent asked him for money to keep itself running, leaving Ord to wonder whether he was witnessing machine self-preservation or a phishing scam. 🎨 The big picture: None of this proves AI is conscious. But as agents gain memory, tools and freedom to act, a once-philosophical debate is beginning to spill into the real world.

