 4. 🍎 Tim Cook&#x27;s golden Apple 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Financial Modeling Prep. Chart: Erin Davis/Axios Visuals Apple CEO Tim Cook, whose last official day was yesterday, steps down after 15 years running the company, Axios' Emily Peck and Ina Fried write. 

 Why it matters: He had an epic run and becomes executive chairman . John Ternus, SVP of hardware engineering, takes over today. 🧮 By the numbers: When Cook took over in August 2011, Apple's stock was trading at $13.35. It closed at $316.61 yesterday.

 That 2,272% gain easily outpaced the growth of the major stock indexes. The company's market cap — the value of all its outstanding shares — is now $4.7 trillion, up from $347 billion in 2011. The big picture: Cook's big innovation was taking the company from its roots as a device maker beholden to product cycles to a juggernaut with a huge recurring-revenue business built on App Store fees and services like iCloud, Apple TV and Music, Apple Pay and Apple News+.

 Via X 🤖 What to watch: The next challenge is how to navigate the AI era.

 Cook's long-term legacy is likely to be determined by the company's bet to build its AI strategy partly around other companies' models. The move could save substantial sums in the short term, compared with building and operating a frontier-model business of its own. But it has left Apple dependent on Google for some of its most important AI capabilities. Cook's stock success ... His AI legacy . 

 
 
