 5. 🎤 Vance courts Jewish Republicans 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Vice President JD Vance speaks yesterday in Sterling Heights, Mich. Photo: Nic Antaya/Getty Images Vice President Vance last night offered an olive branch to Jewish Republicans who have grown wary of his views toward Israel, Axios' Alex Isenstadt reports. 

 During a closed-door appearance before the Republican Jewish Coalition at The Venetian in Las Vegas, Vance called Israel "extremely important to America's national security" and condemned rising antisemitism. Why it matters: Vance's speech is the latest indication he's trying to repair ties with a powerful but skeptical constituency within the GOP ahead of an expected bid for president in 2028.

 Earlier yesterday at the RJC, Ron Dermer, Bibi's close adviser, praised Vance . In a 30-minute Q&amp;A session with former U.S. Sen. Norm Coleman (R-Minn.), Vance recalled discussing antisemitism with the late Charlie Kirk. Vance said Kirk shared his view that "Jew hatred is corrosive to the soul."

 Vance blamed some of the recent rise in antisemitism on immigration, claiming that "a lot" of it was "coming from these Third World immigrants importing these ancient ethnic hatreds." "If you want to bring ethnic hatreds with you, don't bother coming to the United States," Vance said. Vance added that there was "a much deeper cultural symbiosis and harmony" to the U.S. relationship with nations such as the U.K. and Israel, compared to other allies. Behind the scenes: Jewish Republicans have long expressed discomfort over Vance's relationship with Tucker Carlson.

 During his appearance at the RJC, Vance was asked about how the party should deal with right-of-center podcasters who have been critical of Israel, including Carlson, Rogan, Megyn Kelly and Holocaust denier Nick Fuentes. Vance responded that Republicans should aggressively engage with such personalities and make the case for Israel. He didn't address his relationship with Carlson, but defended Rogan and criticized Fuentes ("has nothing but hate in his heart"). The intrigue: Vance's speech highlights a centerpiece of his pre-2028 strategy — appearing before audiences that aren't reflexively supportive.

 Since being tapped as the V.P. nominee in '24, Vance has earned a reputation in Trumpworld for going into hostile media environments and thriving. During his recent book tour, he sparred with liberal-leaning personalities such as Bill Maher and the hosts of "The View." Keep reading . 

 
 
