 8. 🥾 1 for the trail: 2,197 miles at 91 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Dale Sanders, 91, celebrates after completing a difficult section of Mount Katahdin in Maine yesterday. Photo: Robert F. Bukaty/AP In Maine yesterday, 91-year-old Dale Sanders reclaimed the record as the oldest hiker to complete the Appalachian Trail — a 2,197.9-mile trek.

 Sanders, whose trail name is Grey Beard, first set the Appalachian Trail record in 2017 at age 82. Four years later, his 83-year-old friend M.J. Eberhart, aka Nimblewill Nomad, took the title. Data: Appalachian Trail Conservancy The Appalachian Trail Conservancy defines a thru-hike as completing all of the trail's sections in any order within a year, allowing for pauses. Sanders started Sept. 6, 2025, in West Virginia, hopscotched with some brief breaks, and finished yesterday atop Maine's highest peak, where he paused to pray at the sign marking the summit, AP reports . 

 
 
